# Commit messages and PR titles

Use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) with an
imperative subject. Same form for git subjects and GitHub PR titles. This is
project convention, not a CI gate.

## Format

```text
<type>(optional-scope): <imperative summary>

Optional body: why it matters, constraints, or follow-ups.
```

## Types

| Type | Use for |
| --- | --- |
| `feat` | New user-facing or API capability |
| `fix` | Bug fix or incorrect behavior |
| `refactor` | Structure/cleanup with no intended behavior change |
| `docs` | Documentation only |
| `test` | Tests only |
| `perf` | Performance improvement |
| `build` | Docker, packaging, or publish wiring |
| `ci` | GitHub Actions, Jenkins, or other CI pipelines |
| `chore` | Tooling, deps, config, hygiene, or misc non-product work |
| `revert` | Reverts a prior commit (say what/why in subject or body) |

Do **not** invent one-off types. If unsure: `fix` (broken) vs `feat` (new)
vs `chore` (misc).

## Rules

- Imperative mood in the subject (`add`, `fix`, `harden` — not `added` /
  `fixes`).
- Subject / PR title ~72 characters or less; no trailing period; no emoji.
- Scope is optional, lowercase, and short. Omit when unclear.
- Prefer one logical change per commit when practical.
- Subject states the outcome; put file laundry lists and long detail in the
  body.
- Never commit secrets, credentials, tokens, or private personal data.
- Create commits only when the user explicitly asks (unless the consuming
  project has an explicit commit-and-push preference).

## PR titles

- Use the same `<type>(optional-scope): <summary>` form as the commit
  subject.
- Prefer matching the primary commit (or intended squash subject).
- Do not leave GitHub’s default `Update …` / branch-name titles.

## Examples

```text
feat(code): add portable HTML and YAML rules
fix(ci): keep startup probes from killing slow recoveries
docs: require Conventional Commit prefixes
chore(deps): update graylog docker tag to v7.1.6
```
